<h2 id="plain-attributes">Plain attributes</h2>
<p>Not everything a config carries has to be an Orcraft field. A plain class attribute is a legitimate
  way to hand a value to a component when nobody needs to see or edit it - the framework leaves it
  alone entirely:</p>

{{ codeblock.block('class MyConsumeStepConfig(OrcraftTestStepConfig):
	name = OrcraftConfigField.Text(default="My Consume Step")
	sample_rate = OrcraftConfigField.Numeric(default=48000, label="Sample Rate")

	# Plain attributes: filled in by the calling flow, invisible everywhere else.
	calibration = None
	channel_map = ()') }}

<p>A flow sets them the same way it sets declared fields, through <code>run_step</code> keywords or the
  config constructor:</p>

{{ codeblock.block('second_result = self.run_step(
	MyConsumeStep,
	sample_rate=self.config.sample_rate,
	calibration=first_result.calibration,
	channel_map=first_result.channel_map,
)') }}

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th></th>
      <th>Orcraft field</th>
      <th>Plain attribute</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Shown in the run configuration panel</td>
      <td>Yes, when it has a label</td>
      <td>Never</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Logged in the config table</td>
      <td>Yes</td>
      <td>Never</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Validated, coerced, <code>required</code>-checked</td>
      <td>Yes</td>
      <td>No - the value is stored as given</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Settable through <code>run_step</code> keywords</td>
      <td>Yes</td>
      <td>Yes, as long as the attribute exists on the class</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Travels to a remote endpoint with the step</td>
      <td>Yes</td>
      <td>Yes - see <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/steps/#type-fidelity">Type fidelity</a></td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>The one rule is that the attribute must be declared on the class, even if only as <code>None</code>.
  <code>run_step</code> checks that the config has the name before setting it, so a keyword that matches
  nothing raises at the call site instead of silently creating an attribute nobody reads.</p>

<p>Use a plain attribute for data that is an implementation detail between a flow and its steps - a
  calibration table, a handle, a structure too large or too structured to be a GUI input. Use an Orcraft
  field for anything a person should be able to see or change before a run.</p>

<p>Not every inherited field is user-facing. <code>name</code> and <code>description</code>, for
  instance, are configured in code but not editable from the GUI, and fields whose value is at its
  default - a single cycle, an empty matrix - are hidden from the run panel rather than shown as
  clutter.</p>

<p>The two policy fields take a fixed set of values. <code>upload_test_package</code>:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Value</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>always</code></td>
      <td>Upload on every run. The safe choice while the suite is changing.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>if_missing</code></td>
      <td>Upload only when the endpoint does not already have the package. The default.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>never</code></td>
      <td>Never upload - the endpoint is expected to have the package already.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p><code>update_endpoint_app</code>, where <code>defer</code> is available to suites and groups only:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Value</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>always</code></td>
      <td>Update the endpoint application before every run.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>on_mismatch</code></td>
      <td>Update only when the endpoint application does not match the controller. The default for a test.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>never</code></td>
      <td>Leave the endpoint application as it is.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>defer</code></td>
      <td>Express no opinion and leave the decision to the level below. The default for a suite or group.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
